<!-- v3 (2026-11-17, help overhaul): John's structure (intro, What it does,
     How to use this tool, then more) and the shared blocks in ui/css/help.css.
     (v2, 2026-11-15: rewritten for the rebuilt tool; the 1.x text described
     features that never existed.) -->
<p>Over time the MetaForge database collects small mistakes: an artist spelled two ways, a wrong year, a record that shouldn't be there. Database Tools is where you put them right.</p>

<h3>What it does:</h3>
<p>Database Tools lets you correct what MetaForge knows about your artists and albums. You can also remove records that shouldn't be there. It has three tabs: <strong>Artists</strong>, <strong>Albums</strong> and <strong>Remove records</strong>. Before any rename, merge or removal, MetaForge backs up the database.</p>

<section class="steps">
    <h3>How to use this tool:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Choose a tab.</li>
        <li>Find what you want to change: type part of a name in <strong>Find an artist</strong> or <strong>Find an album</strong> and press Enter. If several match, choose one from the list.</li>
        <li>Make your changes, then choose <span class="ui-button">Save changes</span>. "Unsaved changes" shows until you do, and MetaForge asks before you leave the tool.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Some changes affect more than one record: a new name, a merge or a removal. For those, MetaForge shows exactly what will change, with counts, and asks you to confirm first.</p>
</section>

<h3>Artists</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Edit</strong> the name, country, biography and photo (<span class="ui-button">Choose a photo…</span>).</li>
    <li><strong>A new name</strong> is carried through everywhere: the artist's albums, tracks and credits, and the artist tag in the music files. If another artist already has that name, the two are merged.</li>
    <li><span class="ui-button">Merge with another artist…</span> when the same artist is in the database twice under two spellings (for example "The MG's" and "the M.G.'s"). Find the other artist, choose the <strong>Name to keep</strong>, choose <span class="ui-button">Show what changes</span>, and confirm.</li>
    <li><strong>Albums</strong> lists every album the artist is on. Choose one to open it in the Albums tab.</li>
    <li><span class="ui-button">Write a biography in Bio Builder</span> opens <strong class="tool">Bio Builder</strong> with this artist already chosen.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Albums</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Details:</strong> title, album artist, release year, record label, the artist's country, genre and cover (<span class="ui-button">Choose a new cover…</span>). Title, album artist, year and label are also written to the music files' tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Genre:</strong> if the album's tracks have different genres, the list says "Tracks differ: leave them as they are", and saving leaves each track alone. Choose a genre only if every track should have it.</li>
    <li><strong>Tracks:</strong> edit each track's title and original year. On a compilation, each track also has its own artist. <span class="ui-button">More…</span> opens the track's mood, BPM, key, intensity and identifiers; leaving BPM or intensity blank keeps them blank.</li>
    <li><strong>Credits:</strong> the people who played on or made the album. Leave Tracks blank for the whole album, or list track numbers. <span class="ui-button">Add credits from JSON…</span> adds a list of credits at once.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Remove records</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Search for an artist, album, track or credit (<strong>Look in</strong> narrows the search), choose one from <strong>Results</strong>, and choose <span class="ui-button">Remove the selected record…</span>.</li>
    <li>MetaForge shows exactly what will be deleted, and you type the word <strong>remove</strong> to confirm.</li>
    <li>Only database records are removed. <strong>Music files are never touched.</strong></li>
    <li>An artist who performs tracks on other artists' albums can't be removed; merge them into the right artist instead.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Backups</h3>
<p>Before any rename, merge or removal, MetaForge backs up the database, plus any MusicBrainz log it will change. The backup goes in <code>%APPDATA%\MetaForge\backups\database_tools</code>, in a folder named with the date, time and what was done. The five most recent are kept.</p>
